sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2023-competency-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In the 1D artificial embryogeny model, penalizing fitness in proportion to competency (penalty factors 1×10⁻⁷ to 0.5) produced a Baldwin effect: structural-gene fitness kept improving, selection favoured progressively lower competency, and early competency-based gains were assimilated into the structural genes; the authors note real competency costs are unmeasured.";
    dc:type "simulation-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A cost on competency restores a Baldwin effect and genetic assimilation";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Shreesha L, Levin M, “Cellular Competency during Development Alters Evolutionary Dynamics in an Artificial Embryogeny Model”, Entropy (Basel, Switzerland) (2023), doi:10.3390/e25010131; section “3. Results › 3.5. A Fitness Penalty for Competency Leads to Continued Improvement of an Embryo’s Structural Genes”. Scope: In silico 1D artificial embryos with a fitness penalty on competency"
}
